WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.265
Two years ago, we bought this land in Portugal,
and set up our Basekamp.

00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:07.068
We installed a kitchen, and workspace in shipping containers...

00:00:07.068 --> 00:00:08.505
The&nbsp;workspace is now all here...

00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:09.652
Made a small garden...

00:00:09.652 --> 00:00:10.717
Built a toilet...

00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:12.180
Pizza Hut...

00:00:13.125 --> 00:00:14.351
Set up solar energy...

00:00:14.351 --> 00:00:15.420
Cleaned an old well...

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.160
And, set up a water system.

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:18.190
Water!

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.058
I'll show you on the map, what we did in Season&nbsp;1.

00:00:21.058 --> 00:00:27.290
We made pathways, explored the land, discovered ruines,
water, and set up our infrastructure.

00:00:27.290 --> 00:00:28.273
Now, we can live here.

00:00:29.816 --> 00:00:31.782
But, our goal is to live here with many people...

00:00:32.128 --> 00:00:35.584
So, this year we will test to&nbsp;see, 
if everything we made, actually works.

00:00:37.125 --> 00:00:41.182
We will invite many strangers, from around the world,&nbsp;
and see what happens when we put them together.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.620
In this video, you can see how that went,
and everything else we built this year.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.510
Welcome to the Season 2 Recap of Project Kamp!

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.503
We start our journey at Basekamp, right here.

00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:57.419
This&nbsp;is where we spend most of our time.

00:00:58.092 --> 00:01:00.215
First thing, when people came, was getting installed.

00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:01.522
Some came&nbsp;by vans...

00:01:01.522 --> 00:01:05.363
We actually have a big variety of them.

00:01:05.363 --> 00:01:08.448
And, others had to set up their tents, 
in a nice&nbsp;shaded area.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.046
And some, found other places to sleep...

00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:16.815
All right, so, time to start working, and we&nbsp;figured:
"Let's build something fun and basic first."

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.820
We wanted a few wooden decks.

00:01:19.224 --> 00:01:22.859
This was also a&nbsp;good test for us,
to try out how wood behaves in this climate.

00:01:23.155 --> 00:01:25.770
Good to know for all&nbsp;our future structures we want to build.

00:01:27.875 --> 00:01:31.465
So, we made three different decks,
with three&nbsp;different types of wood, and coating.

00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:34.018
So we can see how they behave over time...

00:01:34.709 --> 00:01:40.101
Now, we have decks, where people can enjoy the view,
do yoga, or just relax.

00:01:40.324 --> 00:01:42.220
And, so far, they work pretty well.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.295
Let me&nbsp;show you on the map, where they actually are.

00:01:44.456 --> 00:01:46.770
We installed one on a big rock here...

00:01:47.578 --> 00:01:49.426
One on the field, near the creek...

00:01:49.802 --> 00:01:51.662
And a third one, near Basekamp.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.506
And, this was a nice start!

00:01:53.506 --> 00:01:57.513
Now, we're&nbsp;ready to jump into a bigger project, 
what we were planning on, since we got the&nbsp;land.

00:01:58.293 --> 00:02:00.959
Renovating our sketchy ruin, right here...

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.645
Last year, we cleaned it up,
because it was covered&nbsp;with spiky bushes...

00:02:05.645 --> 00:02:07.000
It was a good discovery!

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.623
- Look at this! We never saw this side of house!

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:13.064
But, the building&nbsp;was rough...

00:02:13.064 --> 00:02:16.634
Burnt down, almost collapsing, and not straight at all.

00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:20.530
Look at the building style of these bricks...

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.076
It's like an artwork.

00:02:24.155 --> 00:02:28.684
This year, we started to renovate it, 
and keep as&nbsp;much of the existing materials as possible.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.172
Just so we have to use less new material...

00:02:31.334 --> 00:02:33.142
Quite a&nbsp;challenge to reuse this.

00:02:33.609 --> 00:02:35.118
We took down the roof....

00:02:35.468 --> 00:02:36.645
Saved all the tiles...

00:02:37.829 --> 00:02:39.137
Remove the bad wall...

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.671
Rebuild back, with the same bricks...

00:02:43.935 --> 00:02:46.566
And now, we had&nbsp;our not very straight base.

00:02:46.835 --> 00:02:48.520
Look at the angles in these walls!

00:02:49.381 --> 00:02:52.487
Now, the rough work was done,
and&nbsp;next, we started to rebuild.

00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:58.200
We installed a wooden frame, that will carry the weight of the roof,
because the brick wall itself is not very reliable...

00:02:58.847 --> 00:03:02.354
Remain keeping that, to close the space,
 and provide&nbsp;some insulation.

00:03:02.677 --> 00:03:05.366
But, the bricks itself, they don't offer much support...

00:03:06.361 --> 00:03:11.502
And, the whole structure&nbsp;got even weaker, 
when we made our last cut in the bricks, to make a big window in front.

00:03:12.201 --> 00:03:14.761
Yeah, this was a bit sketchy, but hey...

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:16.119
- Look at the view!

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.772
Yeah, it's definitely not easier to reuse&nbsp;things...

00:03:18.772 --> 00:03:23.849
But, luckily, everyone on YouTube thought it was a really good idea, 
to keep the existing&nbsp;structure.

00:03:24.172 --> 00:03:26.363
Always nice to get some extra support.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.273
Okay, so, let's leave this ruin for a bit.

00:03:30.273 --> 00:03:31.622
We will finish this later...

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:36.545
Because, around this time, we were walking around,
in this&nbsp;area here on the land of the neighbors.

00:03:36.545 --> 00:03:40.801
And, we heard the sound of water falling,
and&nbsp;discovered something hidden...

00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:44.757
It was hard to see, but behind a lot of spiky bushes and&nbsp;thorns...

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.573
We found an awesome waterfall!

00:03:54.882 --> 00:03:56.018
So, we started clearing it!

00:04:15.084 --> 00:04:19.709
We found beautiful big old rocks stacked as&nbsp;a wall,
and discovered some sort of a gate...

00:04:20.648 --> 00:04:23.590
We asked the neighbor, if it's&nbsp;actually okay,
if we clean all&nbsp;of this.

00:04:23.778 --> 00:04:24.682
And, he loved it!

00:04:25.005 --> 00:04:26.823
He said it's been a while, since he came here.

00:04:27.119 --> 00:04:36.389
 

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.920
He also said the gate was made for irrigation,&nbsp;
and that there was a big lagoon, behind the door.&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.622
We wanted to see that, and started cleaning up there.

00:04:45.364 --> 00:04:47.231
Seems like a big open space...

00:04:47.742 --> 00:04:51.402
And, after clearing it, it was much easier
 to see the potential of the&nbsp;space.

00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:56.167
We could take a nice dip down here, but not yet on the top part...

00:04:56.167 --> 00:05:00.000
Because, we found out the&nbsp;gate 
was completely stuck, so there's no water...

00:05:01.061 --> 00:05:04.254
And, we tried jacks and grinders, but it won't move...

00:05:04.362 --> 00:05:06.043
So, we can't fill up the Lagoon yet...

00:05:06.043 --> 00:05:08.278
You know I was quite disappointed...

00:05:09.084 --> 00:05:13.422
It's frustrating, because we&nbsp;tried a lot of the instruments...

00:05:15.144 --> 00:05:17.352
So, we decided for a more drastic approach.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.621
Yep, we put ketchup on it!

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.751
No, that also didn't work...

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:25.164
We cut the thing in half, and took it out...

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:27.769
Brought it to the workspace...

00:05:27.769 --> 00:05:29.685
Welded it back together...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.842
Added some reinforcement...

00:05:32.272 --> 00:05:33.732
Gave it a paint job...

00:05:34.243 --> 00:05:36.178
And, put it back in.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:45.576
Seems to work!

00:05:46.571 --> 00:05:49.664
Now, we just have to&nbsp;wait for rain to come,
 at the end of the year...

00:05:50.675 --> 00:05:54.360
So, while waiting, let's go back to the&nbsp;Basekamp, 
where we made some upgrades.

00:05:54.917 --> 00:05:58.066
In Season 1, we built this clay oven&nbsp;and a pizza hut.

00:05:58.066 --> 00:06:01.022
And this year, we integrated actually a good weekly routine.

00:06:01.022 --> 00:06:04.397
Where we bake&nbsp;pizzas, and our sour-dough bread, every Friday.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.330
But, doing this, we also realized the space&nbsp;
needed an upgrade, with a proper table.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.780
So, with some old, not very straight&nbsp;wood,
we made a strong wooden frame.

00:06:18.042 --> 00:06:19.914
And, we added a piece of granite on top.

00:06:19.914 --> 00:06:22.163
A&nbsp;rock that you find a lot, in this region.

00:06:22.324 --> 00:06:25.881
For painting the wood, we used the opportunity to try&nbsp;a natural ink.

00:06:25.881 --> 00:06:28.758
Made from acorns, rust, and water we found on the land.

00:06:29.081 --> 00:06:32.227
It created a deep black color, which&nbsp;looks pretty cool.

00:06:32.227 --> 00:06:37.225
And, the table gives us a nice big surface to work with flour, 
and doubles as an&nbsp;office during the week.

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:40.473
It's good to see our pizzas getting better!

00:06:40.473 --> 00:06:45.455
From a first setup, with a shovel and a tarp,
to a proper structure, tools and table.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.568
Another upgrade we needed, was the shower...

00:06:48.568 --> 00:06:52.610
The one&nbsp;we set up last year was temporary,
 and made from bended branches laying around...

00:06:52.887 --> 00:06:54.351
So, it was a bit&nbsp;exposed.

00:06:54.666 --> 00:06:56.542
- Which means, there isn't much privacy...

00:06:56.542 --> 00:06:59.723
Yeah, especially with more people around this&nbsp;year,
it became a bit of a thing...

00:07:00.557 --> 00:07:04.013
So, we took it down, and built a new version,
 with more straight&nbsp;planks.

00:07:04.013 --> 00:07:06.084
So, we don't have all the gaps in between...

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.000
And, for the water catchment&nbsp;basin, on the bottom...

00:07:09.377 --> 00:07:11.028
- That's disgusting...

00:07:12.584 --> 00:07:16.063
We tried making it with this new kind of&nbsp;cement: 
magnesium-phosphate.

00:07:16.383 --> 00:07:18.785
Because, it's waterproof, and suited for the shower.

00:07:19.081 --> 00:07:24.000
We want to explore alternative to the standard Portland cement, 
since it's&nbsp;a big carbon emitter in the world...

00:07:25.454 --> 00:07:29.470
So, we did a bunch of tests to find the right ratio,
and mixture, and made a basin.

00:07:30.896 --> 00:07:34.178
This was only a small little research, 
but it's an interesting material though.

00:07:34.555 --> 00:07:38.956
We will continue to see how it behaves in real life, 
and let you know in the research module.

00:07:39.978 --> 00:07:41.567
For now, we have a more private shower.

00:07:41.567 --> 00:07:43.854
We also added a little bench outside...

00:07:43.854 --> 00:07:47.510
And, installed&nbsp;a sink, from an old washer for the clothes.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.189
Yeah, I don't know how you call these&nbsp;things, but it works.

00:07:52.189 --> 00:07:53.259
- This works pretty well...

00:07:53.824 --> 00:07:57.925
So, when summer came, we noticed a more&nbsp;
crucial thing popping up, to research.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.220
Apparently, last year, Season&nbsp;1, for us was quite rainy...

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.445
This year is much drier, and hotter.

00:08:04.983 --> 00:08:05.841
- It's hot!

00:08:05.841 --> 00:08:09.838
Which, in this area, means forest fires, and lots of them.

00:08:13.394 --> 00:08:15.302
On this app, you can see where they are...

00:08:15.598 --> 00:08:18.000
Everywhere&nbsp;around us, and quite scary actually...

00:08:18.511 --> 00:08:21.744
Especially, knowing that our own land burned down, five years ago.

00:08:21.744 --> 00:08:24.568
This makes it very real, that it would likely happen again...

00:08:25.186 --> 00:08:28.500
But, we realized, we don't&nbsp;know much about it, 
or how to prevent it.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.636
So, we went to visit a local fire department...

00:08:31.636 --> 00:08:33.878
Ask the firefighters for tips...

00:08:33.878 --> 00:08:36.909
We did an interview with a professor, focused on wildfires...

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:39.206
And, visited&nbsp;a few big fires around.

00:08:40.013 --> 00:08:47.307
We learned a lot, but the main take we got from it:
there's a lot of&nbsp;work ahead for us, to become more fire resilient.

00:08:48.869 --> 00:08:52.908
Yeah, we needed to think more&nbsp;about this,
but in the summer, it&nbsp;was just too hot...

00:08:53.209 --> 00:08:56.141
Our creek dried&nbsp;up, which apparently rarely happens.

00:08:56.921 --> 00:09:01.016
And, here you can see a green Basekamp&nbsp;in the winter,
and a brown version in the summer.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.970
It was really hard to work, and think, with the heat.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.854
Yeah, I think Colleen sums it up pretty well here...

00:09:06.988 --> 00:09:07.596
- No can do...

00:09:07.892 --> 00:09:09.125
- We cannot do construction...

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:13.221
So, we took a swim at the local river, 
to cool down&nbsp;from the heat.

00:09:13.259 --> 00:09:15.480
And, made a plan&nbsp;on what to do about these fires...

00:09:29.719 --> 00:09:33.801
So, the land has many green areas around, 
for its&nbsp;vegetation to grow, and this is nice.

00:09:33.801 --> 00:09:35.129
Well, sort of...

00:09:35.129 --> 00:09:38.171
They can get really dry in summer, and become&nbsp;a big fire!

00:09:38.763 --> 00:09:40.823
It's basically a fuel for fire.

00:09:41.453 --> 00:09:45.744
But, this all does depend on what kind of trees, 
and&nbsp;bushes are actually growing there.

00:09:45.879 --> 00:09:48.845
So, we started by mapping out all the trees,
that we have on the land.

00:09:48.845 --> 00:09:53.470
Walking around on 10 hectares,
and identifying every native tree that we see growing.

00:09:53.739 --> 00:09:55.229
Just&nbsp;to get an overview...

00:09:55.229 --> 00:09:56.955
All the black dots are native trees.

00:09:57.251 --> 00:09:59.042
We found a lot in this area!

00:09:59.803 --> 00:10:02.673
And, we like&nbsp;them a lot,
because they are more fire resilient.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.686
And, the coolest one is a cork oak.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.490
This&nbsp;one is most resilient, because they have a thick bark, 
that protects them from fire.

00:10:10.714 --> 00:10:13.080
This&nbsp;one actually burned out in the previous fire...

00:10:13.214 --> 00:10:15.039
You still see the ash on my hand!

00:10:15.685 --> 00:10:19.736
But, unlike all the&nbsp;other trees,
it survived, because of this thick bark.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:24.360
But, they grow slow, so we're happy that 
we still have a bunch of them on top of the rocks.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.120
Anyway, next to Basekamp, almost every&nbsp;tree 
is a mimosa, and they burn fast...

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.626
So, one of the main reasons to get rid&nbsp;of mimosas,
is to bring back the native&nbsp;trees.

00:10:40.666 --> 00:10:42.959
But, also, to make the land a&nbsp;bit more fire resilient...

00:10:42.959 --> 00:10:45.344
Because, as you can see, they burn very fast...

00:10:45.344 --> 00:10:47.505
And, this is a fresh cut of mimosa trees.

00:10:47.774 --> 00:10:51.666
We asked some advice, from our neighbor&nbsp;Antonio,
on what to do with all these trees.

00:10:51.666 --> 00:10:56.307
 

00:10:56.791 --> 00:10:58.933
 

00:10:58.933 --> 00:10:59.840
Our neighbor's right.

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:02.251
Our Kamp is surrounded by&nbsp;potential fire fuel.

00:11:02.251 --> 00:11:05.787
So, we started to clean up that big mimosa patch, 
behind the Basekamp.

00:11:05.787 --> 00:11:08.039
Reducing potential fuel for fire.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.660
We gathered the wood,
that could&nbsp;be used as firewood, for winter...

00:11:16.765 --> 00:11:19.062
And, we use a wood chipper to chip the&nbsp;branches.

00:11:19.815 --> 00:11:21.958
We have a lot of mimosa on the land...

00:11:23.334 --> 00:11:26.314
There's going to be an ongoing battle,
to live in&nbsp;harmony with them...

00:11:26.717 --> 00:11:29.170
It's really impressive to see how fast they grow!

00:11:29.359 --> 00:11:31.733
These trees started growing&nbsp;after the last fire!

00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:33.949
Look how big it got in five years!

00:11:34.299 --> 00:11:36.344
Local people around us hate them for that.

00:11:36.344 --> 00:11:39.155
It's just an annoying useless tree, like a weed...

00:11:39.585 --> 00:11:41.465
But, we actually got curious to it!

00:11:41.465 --> 00:11:42.842
There must&nbsp;be some use for it...

00:11:43.246 --> 00:11:45.628
So, we wanted to see what we can actually make with it.

00:11:46.247 --> 00:11:47.958
So, we did some wood&nbsp;carving...

00:11:48.684 --> 00:11:51.309
Took the bark, and used it to weave baskets...

00:11:52.515 --> 00:11:55.273
Chopped some chunks,
to make stairs in our&nbsp;bumpy terrain...

00:11:56.349 --> 00:11:58.988
And, made a bar stool, to set at the new pizza table.

00:11:58.988 --> 00:12:00.797
To make sure people can work happy.

00:12:00.958 --> 00:12:06.621
Now that the stool is finished, Martijn can sit at the proper height, 
with a straight back, and work happily!

00:12:07.784 --> 00:12:12.430
Overall, the clearing was a big action,
but nothing compared to the size of&nbsp;our entire land!

00:12:13.103 --> 00:12:15.577
All of this is mimosa trees -- yikes!

00:12:15.846 --> 00:12:18.249
So, this will be a long-term goal to work&nbsp;towards...

00:12:18.732 --> 00:12:20.988
Okay, for now, time to finish up the ruin!

00:12:21.338 --> 00:12:23.654
We're going to close it up, and install the roof.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.566
For that, we needed to get insulation material...

00:12:26.874 --> 00:12:29.892
And, we love the heat resistant cork oak tree, you&nbsp;saw before.

00:12:30.377 --> 00:12:34.177
This is a great insulation material, 
and actually grows a lot in Portugal.

00:12:34.177 --> 00:12:40.484
They become&nbsp;big old trees, and every nine years, the bark is harvested,
 and can be processed into insulation&nbsp;material.

00:12:40.914 --> 00:12:43.327
and the sounds of this harvesting were amazing

00:12:43.757 --> 00:12:48.550
Silence, and the only thing you'll&nbsp;hear 
is squeaky noises from the cork, and the axe.

00:12:56.084 --> 00:12:59.351
And the cool thing, is that the tree stays&nbsp;alive, when harvested.

00:12:59.351 --> 00:13:01.485
This cork gets shredded, and processed.

00:13:01.485 --> 00:13:07.300
And, what makes expanded cork&nbsp;even cooler, is that it's all natural,
and no glue is needed, to make these panels.

00:13:07.569 --> 00:13:09.863
Just heat, and&nbsp;pressure, that binds it together.

00:13:09.863 --> 00:13:10.592
Pretty cool!

00:13:11.453 --> 00:13:14.226
So, we got some for the ruin, 
and started to put it on the&nbsp;roof.

00:13:14.226 --> 00:13:16.569
We put the cork on the wooden beams...

00:13:18.703 --> 00:13:20.262
Added the waterproof layer...

00:13:20.314 --> 00:13:23.208
And, added those&nbsp;old reused tiles, from before, on top.

00:13:23.208 --> 00:13:24.136
Now, it's waterproof!

00:13:24.458 --> 00:13:25.807
Next: the interior!

00:13:26.042 --> 00:13:29.527
We installed some old&nbsp;windows, that we got 
from a local guy, that had them left-over.

00:13:30.404 --> 00:13:32.541
We placed the wooden frame on the walls...

00:13:32.971 --> 00:13:35.312
And, insulated the whole thing with our beloved cork.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:39.974
Next, we finished the walls with wood panels...

00:13:40.916 --> 00:13:43.396
And, used the same panels to make the floor.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:52.170
Finally, we added some recycled plastic sockets, 
made&nbsp;with Precious Plastic machines...

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:54.256
And, used old-school&nbsp;light switches.

00:13:55.063 --> 00:13:58.681
And, when it was all done, we added&nbsp;the stove, 
so we could heat it up in the winter.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:06.814
This will be a big upgrade, 
compared&nbsp;to the steamy van, last year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.782 --> 00:14:12.018
And finally, we also built a veranda&nbsp;outside.

00:14:12.314 --> 00:14:17.236
This is so we can enter the house easily, 
but also have a nice&nbsp;place to sit, and enjoy the view.

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:35.580
Here we have a nice view,&nbsp;to Antonio's and Fernanda's house.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.460
We actually invited to her, 
so she&nbsp;could have a look in the house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.262 --> 00:14:43.577
 

00:14:43.577 --> 00:14:46.395
She used to live in this beautiful, 
orange brick, when she was younger.

00:14:46.928 --> 00:14:48.745
 

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.640
Good to hear she likes it!

00:14:50.969 --> 00:14:54.052
 

00:14:54.084 --> 00:14:54.802
 

00:14:54.968 --> 00:14:56.136
 

00:14:57.011 --> 00:14:58.885
 

00:14:59.594 --> 00:15:00.303
 

00:15:01.593 --> 00:15:02.217
 

00:15:02.883 --> 00:15:07.380
Talking about neighbors, around the end of the year,
they often ask us for some help with harvesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:08.825
Things like potatoes...

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:12.875
This is a big job for them, as they're getting older, 
but we have lots of energy!

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:14.220
And, it's fun!

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.227
At the end, we always get a little&nbsp;homemade snack.

00:15:16.354 --> 00:15:20.279
There are also a few other projects around in the region,
 where we source some of our&nbsp;veggies.

00:15:20.493 --> 00:15:22.444
We like to visit, and help, if needed.

00:15:22.579 --> 00:15:27.771
In these years, we realized it's very useful to have 
a regional network, and learn from each other's experiences.

00:15:28.417 --> 00:15:29.274
- Thank you!

00:15:29.274 --> 00:15:31.417
- Well, if you're&nbsp;happy, I'm happy!

00:15:31.552 --> 00:15:32.769
Okay, so, back to the map!

00:15:32.903 --> 00:15:35.945
We still have some stuff to show you, 
our neighbors&nbsp;actually told us about.

00:15:36.214 --> 00:15:39.645
They often mentioned some things, from the old days,
that used to be on&nbsp;the land...

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:45.063
And, one of them was an old building somewhere here,
between these bushes, and it's&nbsp;hard to see.

00:15:45.063 --> 00:15:46.968
So, we cleaned it up, to get a better look.

00:15:47.156 --> 00:15:49.706
And, we found this very old collapsed&nbsp;ruin...

00:15:49.706 --> 00:15:52.020
It requires some imagination to see it...

00:15:56.044 --> 00:15:58.067
- A piece of metal that turned the door...

00:15:58.067 --> 00:16:00.388
I'm not sure yet what to do with it, but...

00:16:00.388 --> 00:16:02.477
Good to know there's a pile of stones here.

00:16:02.477 --> 00:16:06.000
And, we made a technical drawing of it, 
but no future plans yet.

00:16:06.875 --> 00:16:12.504
So, the ruin was there, and another thing they&nbsp;mentioned, 
is that we have a well, on the bottom&nbsp;of our land, around here.

00:16:12.504 --> 00:16:14.998
And, we searched&nbsp;before, but we never found it...

00:16:14.998 --> 00:16:17.343
And, they keep telling us it should be in this area.

00:16:17.693 --> 00:16:20.990
So, after some&nbsp;searching, we finally found it, 
under a pile of wood...

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:22.716
- Hey! Over here!

00:16:22.931 --> 00:16:26.412
And, we figured: let's clean it up,&nbsp;
before the rain comes, so it can fill up.

00:16:27.919 --> 00:16:30.668
And, jobs like this can go really fast, with many hands!

00:16:31.098 --> 00:16:34.292
We&nbsp;cleared the area around it, 
and emptied the dirt that was inside.

00:16:34.965 --> 00:16:38.588
Looks like a pretty cool old&nbsp;well,
which I guess will be useful in the future.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:44.265
So, at this point, the dry summer is over,
and&nbsp;the winter is starting...

00:16:44.265 --> 00:16:46.140
Which means: rain, lots of it...

00:16:48.147 --> 00:16:51.697
Really good to see the plants coming back to&nbsp;life,
 and the creek flowing again.

00:16:52.959 --> 00:16:54.964
And, the new well is filling up with water...

00:16:54.964 --> 00:16:55.902
- Feels like a good&nbsp;start!

00:16:56.225 --> 00:16:59.104
So, this also means we can test the door from the waterfall!

00:16:59.584 --> 00:17:01.583
We can close it up, to see what&nbsp;happens.

00:17:03.493 --> 00:17:05.615
It's really good seeing the lagoon fill up!

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:10.685
It stores a lot of water, as you can see.

00:17:10.685 --> 00:17:14.298
But, you really notice this, 
when you&nbsp;open it up, and it all comes out.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:24.849
Yeah, this action actually flushed away our GoPro,&nbsp;
now it's...

00:17:24.849 --> 00:17:27.193
- Somewhere are there but there are a lot of brumbles...

00:17:28.699 --> 00:17:30.538
And, we thought THIS was a lot of water!

00:17:30.538 --> 00:17:34.073
But, after a few months of rain, the waterfall was on steroids!

00:17:34.234 --> 00:17:37.663
Instead of a waterfall, it became&nbsp;more like 20 waterfalls!

00:17:38.067 --> 00:17:39.625
Such a cool place...

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.918
So, that was it -- everything we made so far!

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.701
Now, Season 3 is starting,
with a new video coming every monday!

00:17:48.867 --> 00:17:50.738
Subscribe on Youtube, to follow our journey...

00:17:50.738 --> 00:17:53.613
And, support us on Patreon, 
to see videos one week ahead! 

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.930
Thanks to everyone watching this!
 Project Kamp would not be possible without you!

